The word project comes from the
Latin proicere, meaning “to throw
forward,” as in to throw the dice.
And for the first half millennium
or so, the word implied a gamble
or mad scheme, not a sensible plan
marked out with Gantt charts or
PERT graphs. Vampires tend to
remain conservative in their word
usage, and the Camarilla’s institutional
inertia and conservatism
keep much of the old skepticism
when they refer to younger vampires’
projects. Given the number
of things that can go wrong when
bloody-minded predators plan
them, the Camarilla has a point.
However, players often want
their characters to accomplish
goals and affect the outside world,
but don’t want to focus every scene
of the game on a scheme to undermine
public housing policy (so
many more tasty homeless to feed
from) or bankrupt a chain of coffee
shops run by a rival. For this sort
of subplot or sub rosa activity, the
Project system offers the Storyteller
a way to ask the elders’ recurring
question: “Are you sure you want
to do that?” and to monitor the
action when the players inevitably
answer “Of course! What could go
wrong?”
Launching a Project
To launch a project, the player (or
players) must first describe their
goal in story terms: Win the heart
of the chief harpy, destroy the
credit of the biggest bank in the
city, take over the vice squad, uncover
the meaning of a mysterious
glyph spray-painted on the wall of
their haven.
Then, the player and Storyteller
need to come up with the
mechanical representation of their
goal. This usually takes the form of
one or more dots in a Background
or other Trait. The harpy would
become a Mawla, destroying the
bank might add to their Resources
(by selling short) or just subtract
Resources from a hated rival, taking
over the vice squad provides
dots of Influence, and figuring out
the glyph might provide dots on a
Loresheet or allow the purchase of
a Blood Sorcery Ritual.
Vampires often use projects to
change a vessel’s Resonance and
intensify their temperament over
the long term. One dot changes
Resonance and increases it to
Intense, while two dots changes a
Resonance and adds a Dyscrasia.
The number of dots the project
adds if successful is called the
project’s Scope.
project increment
Finally, the player and Storyteller
decide on the increment for the
project: days, weeks, fortnights,
months, years, sevenals, decades,
mortal generations, centuries, etc.
One increment should roughly
equal the project’s likely time span
divided by ten. Thus a project that
culminates in a year has a roughly
monthly increment.
A project less than ten days
long should just be an extended
test (p. 293).
launch roll
If the Storyteller expects that
nurturing or protecting the project
might take on a major role in the
chronicle going forward, or that
the scene can provide dramatic
juice or relief, the troupe should
play out a scene or even a chapter
dedicated to stage one, laying the
groundwork of their plot.
At the end of that period, the
player makes the Launch roll to
start the project. The Storyteller
tells them which dice pool to use
for the Launch roll: for most
projects, the launch roll is Skill +
Background. Courting the harpy
might be Subterfuge + Status, destroying
the bank requires Finance
+ Resources, taking over the vice
squad uses Politics + Influence, figuring
out the glyph calls for Occult
+ Loresheet (or Blood Sorcery, or
a magician Ally). As with mortals,
vampires need leverage to get leverage.
Temporary dots from Boons,
Memoriam, and other sources
might also bolster the Launch roll.
The launch roll is a simple
test against a Difficulty equal to
the project’s Scope + 2. The player
cannot spend Willpower or use a
Blood Surge on the launch roll. The
Storyteller may allow the player
to use Disciplines on the launch
roll, especially if the player aims
the project at mortals rather than
fellow Kindred.
On a critical win, the player
does not have to commit any of
their current dots to the project.
On a regular win, the player
must commit a number of dots
equal to the project’s Scope + 1,
