minus their margin on the launch
roll.
EXAMPLE
Istvan wishes to build a drug
empire in Hamburg to get five
dots in Resources. He already has
Resources ••, so his project Scope is
three dots. Given that he only has
Resources ••, he asks the Storyteller
if he can leverage his Influence •••
in the docks instead. The Storyteller
agrees and sets the Launch
die pool as Streetwise + Influence.
Istvan calls in a boon from the
Primogen to bump him up from
seven (Streetwise 4 + Influence 3) to
eight dice, against a Difficulty of 5
(2 + Scope 3). He rolls well, getting
six successes. He can subtract one
dot (his margin was 1 over the
Difficulty) from the stake, for a net
stake of three dots. Rather than
play without any Influence, Istvan
stakes one dot of Resources and two
dots of his Influence on this plot.
(The boon dot disappeared when he
spent it on the Launch roll.) He’ll
have some lean times and have to
live by his wits for a while, but it
all pays off at the end.
The minimum commitment, or
stake, on a regular win is one dot.
Dots committed to a project stake
cannot be used in play until the
project ends one way or another –
the player has those dots “tied up”
in the project. As a rule of thumb,
the dots in the stake should be
the same type as dots in the goal,
but it’s easy to imagine gambling
Resources to get Influence, or some
other combination. Storytellers
should be wary of players offering
“empty dots” that don’t come up
in play or don’t lose effectiveness
at lower levels (like Mask, or some
Loresheets) as stakes.
On a failure, the player can try
again to start the project, with a
launch roll Difficulty one higher.
The time isn’t right, someone
talked who shouldn’t, something
went wrong.
On a total failure, the character
has a new enemy, or an energized
and motivated old enemy. The
Storyteller may decide that these
enemies destroy or estrange some
part of the character’s Background,
costing them a dot or more.
If the Storyteller agrees, players
can cooperate on a Launch roll,
either by using a Coterie Background
or by using regular Teamwork
(p. 122). However, multiple
players take multiple risks: the
stakes and losses must come from
every player involved in the launch
roll, even if that means increasing
their cost. If four characters
helped, and the stake is three dots,
all four vampires need to put up
one dot.
goal roll
Assuming a win, the Storyteller
sets a Project Die at 10. Rather
than keep a loose die on the table
when it’s not being used, they can
also just write the Project Die
number for a given scheme down
in their notes along with its Scope
and intended mechanical effect. A
Storyteller with multiple conspiracies
to track likely keeps a master
list on paper or in their phone, but
nothing beats the visual and tactile
sensation of clicking a die over to
the next face when the time comes
right.
Because indeed, the Project
Die decrements by one after each
increment, counting down from 10
to 9 to 8 … and eventually, to 1.
If the players (or characters)
become impatient, they can make a
Goal roll at any time. The Storyteller
tells you your dice pool,
which may or may not be the same
as your Launch roll dice pool. (You
can use “staked” dots in the Goal
roll, since they’re already dedicated
to that goal.) However, the Goal
roll is a conflict roll, against a dice
pool equal to the current value of
the Project Die: for example, if you
try for success when the Project
Die shows 6, you roll against a pool
of six dice.
Again, you cannot spend
Willpower, Blood Surges, or (usually)
Disciplines on this roll. Also,
the plotter gets no criticals on the
Goal roll: each 10 only counts as
one regular success. Worse yet, the
opposition criticals do count –
call it the house advantage for the
status quo.
If the player wins, they do
damage to the Project Die, decrementing
it by the number of
extra successes they rolled. If the
Project Die decrements below 1,
the project suddenly succeeds. The
Storyteller comes up with an ingame
reason for this dramatic shift
of events, which they may or may
not share with the player.
If the player loses, they take
