4 1 9
damage as losses to their Background
dots, starting with the
stake. If the loss reduces their stake
to 0, the project suddenly collapses.
(The exception: a player who
rolled a critical on their Launch
roll can keep a project in play even
after losing a Goal roll.) Again, the
Storyteller comes up with an ingame
reason for this failure, which
they may or may not share with
the player.
EXAMPLE:
Istvan makes a Goal roll when the
Project Die shows 5. He rolls his
Streetwise + Influence (plus his one
staked Resource dot, for a total
of eight dice) against the opposition
pool of five dice. He rolls six
successes, against four for the opposition.
He decrements the Project
Die by his margin of 2, lowering it
to 3. If the opposition had rolled a
critical, however, he would have
lost four dots – his whole stake,
plus one more dot (likely from
Influence) – and the project would
have collapsed.
Like stakes, damage must be shared
among all the players who assisted
with a project’s Launch roll, or
taken from the Coterie’s Backgrounds.
Ending a Project
The initiator of a project can always
end it, although they may not
be able to call off any enemies they
made with their plot so easily.
The Storyteller may still make
the player pay Experience for dots
gained through a Project – the Project
system provides the in-game
action component of an improvement
only. Some Storytellers may
allow a price break on the goal’s
Experience cost, depending on the
degree of success, the quality of
the players’ planning and roleplaying
surrounding the project, and
the general looseness of the game’s
Experience economy.
The Longue Durée
To properly profit from a project
lasting decades or centuries, the
characters need to have already
launched their plot decades or
centuries ago.
To have already launched a
Longue Durée project in the past,
the characters need to play out a
launch chapter in Memoriam
(p. 311).
This uses the same rules as
above, but the characters’ Goal
dots from Memoriam become part
(or all) of the Launch roll die pool,
and of the stake going forward. The
Project Die can only be decremented
in the present by the number of
Goal dots achieved in Memoriam:
if you want a Project Die at 4 in
the present, you need to gain six
Goal dots in Memoriam and spend
them all on the project.
Damage done by a lost Goal
roll in the present comes off
present Backgrounds, not off the
Memoriam stake.
Opposition Projects
Player characters are hardly the
only Kindred with tangled knots
of long-running schemes. They
can detect, hamper, and even
hijack their opponents’ (or allies’)
projects nearly as easily as starting
their own.
The Storyteller needs to determine
the current Project die value
for the project, and at least a rough
dot value for its Scope. When in
doubt, roll a die for the Project
die value; roll again and divide the
result in half (rounded up) for its
Scope.
detecting an
opposition project
Discovering that a plot exists at all
may require specific in-game actions
from the characters. Determining
how far it has progressed
– getting the correct value of the
Project die – definitely requires
investigation, or at least an informant.
On screen, an enemy Project
die decrements at least once during
the story, because an intensifying
conspiracy makes for better drama.
Dramatic considerations also make
it more likely that a noticeable
project is dangerous or unfriendly
to the player characters. Otherwise,
why bring it on stage at all?
affecting an
opposition project
Player character coteries can affect
supporting characters’ Project die
by dedicating a chapter (or a story,
for longer-term or larger-magnitude
projects) to advancing or delaying
its plot. Dramatic law means
RULEBOOK 4 2 0
the coterie turns out to be critical
(or potentially critical) to whatever
plot they focus the story on – and
realistically, a group of vampires
can indeed do an awful lot of damage
if they work together. It’s up to
the Storyteller to help them decide
a suitably powerful in-game action
for the coterie to take, even if it’s
of the “for want of a nail the battle
was lost” or “tiny pebble triggering
the avalanche” sort of powerful
action.
Successful interference increments
(or decrements) the Project
die by one or more, at the Storyteller’s
discretion. Critical wins on
critical rolls might even shortcircuit
a project entirely, or (less
plausibly) bring it to completion
early.
Interfering with an ongoing
project almost certainly invites reprisal
from someone powerful: the
target of the plot if the coterie tries
to advance it, or the author of the
plot if the coterie tries to derail it.
using an opposition project
Instead of derailing or accelerating
a scheme, often a coterie prefers
to hijack it for their own purposes.
The specific systems in play
vary with the coterie’s wishes and
the existing conspiracy’s design:
draining money from a financial
conspiracy requires skills that
false-flagging cultists from an
underground church does not, and
vice versa.
However, in general the characters
must first be in a reasonable
position to access or influence
the conspiracy’s elements. The
Storyteller judges whether they
meet this requirement just by
discovering the plot, or must also
maneuver themselves into such a
position with a story action or a
roll or both.
The Storyteller then sets the
player character Goal roll dice
pool; since the characters don’t
need to have laid any groundwork,
this pool can be a normal
Skill Pool such as Manipulation
+ Finance or Occult or Politics
or Streetwise, or something else
entirely, depending on the nature
of the project.
Then, they need to make a (Difficulty
5) hard extended test
(p. 294) with a Target Number
equal to 15 minus the value of the
Project Die. Failures during this extended
test alert the owners of the
project that someone is interfering
with it; a total failure tells them
specifically who dares to interfere.
Once successfully entwined
with the project, the characters can
either loot it or hijack it. Looting
a project transfers half of its Scope
in dots (rounded up) to the coterie,
and requires no further roll: the
extended test represents getting
enough access to loot the project.
Hijacking a project requires an
immediate Goal roll, just as if the
characters had originated the project.
(The Storyteller may change
this dice pool up to put the player
characters’ Backgrounds into play,
or to better suit the narrative.) If
successful, they get the benefit of
the project; if they fail, they lose
the indicated number of Background
dots.
Whatever the outcome, the
characters now have another
enemy to go along with their new
hoard of dots. If their new enemy
defeats them over the course of
that story or the next, story, the
characters lose their looted or
hijacked dots ■
